          "text": "Clinical Indication:\nHistory of combined generalized and focal epilepsy with generalized tonic-clonic seizures beginning at age 39, currently occurring approximately 5 times per month. Epilepsy is drug-resistant and has a genetic etiology associated with an STXBP1 c.902A>G variant. Current antiseizure medications are oxcarbazepine and valproate.\n\nTechnique:\nRoutine scalp EEG recording using the international 10–20 electrode placement system with standard referential and bipolar montages.\n\nFindings:\nSpecific background, interictal, and ictal EEG findings were not provided; therefore, no electrographic abnormalities or seizures are asserted.\n\nImpression:\nNo electrographic interpretation can be made from the supplied information. The clinical history of combined generalized and focal epilepsy and generalized tonic-clonic seizures is not, by itself, an EEG finding and does not establish ictal localization.",
